merge(W + 'entities/barack-obama.md', """## Role in the 2024 Biden replacement debate

After the June 2024 [[2024-presidential-debate|presidential debate]], [[tucker-carlson|Tucker Carlson]] reported that a "reliable source" told him Obama was supporting an open Democratic convention.[N] Badlands contributor [[ashe-in-america|Ashe in America]], noting the widespread belief in the Truth Community that Obama was the one actually calling the shots behind [[joe-biden|Joe Biden]], argued that his direction would carry weight and that if the report were true, Democratic politicians were brazenly lying to their voters while deciding what to do next.[N] Contributor [[burning-bright|Burning Bright]] read the situation differently, observing that Obama and [[hillary-clinton|Hillary Clinton]] publicly stood pat with Biden and set the narrative for the "Old Guard" even as friendly media outlets turned on him — behavior he interpreted, through [[devolution]] framing, as fear that they might be unable to replace Biden rather than fear that they would have to.[N]
""")

merge(W + 'entities/mohammed-bin-salman.md', """## Red Sea tourism ambitions and the Trump brand

Badlands coverage of the July 2024 announcement of [[trump-tower-jeddah|Trump Tower Jeddah]] tied the project to Mohammed bin Salman's stated goal of turning the Red Sea coast into the next French Riviera as an iconic tourist destination.[N] Contributor [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] argued that bringing the Trump brand to the Jeddah waterfront through [[dar-global|Dar Global]] was a major step toward that objective.[N] The same commentary noted that Saudi Arabia announced on July 1, 2024 the discovery of seven new oil and gas deposits in the Empty Quarter, the barren southern desert bordering Yemen and Oman long considered useless by all but the Bedouin.[N]
""")

merge(W + 'concepts/abraham-accords.md', """## Interpretation as a counterinsurgency instrument (2024)

Writing in the July 2, 2024 Badlands News Brief, [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] restated his view that the Accords were wielded as a political weapon intended to ignite a peaceful counterinsurgency across the region and within Israel against [[benjamin-netanyahu|Benjamin Netanyahu]] and the Israeli Deep State, and argued that this was visibly happening.[N] In his account the Accords got the Arab states "all playing the same sheet of music" after decades of infighting over what to do about Israel and perceived Zionist aggression.[N] He cited as evidence Arab states working to keep Israel well-provisioned while Iran-linked proxy militias attacked Red Sea shipping, Arab participation in shielding Israel from Iran's missile barrage, and [[hezbollah|Hezbollah]] issuing specific advance warnings about which targets it would strike if the IDF invaded Lebanon — behavior he called a bizarre strategy for anyone actually intending to fight.[N] He credited Saudi foreign minister [[faisal-bin-farhan|Faisal bin Farhan]] with running the diplomacy, working every channel and speaking daily with members of the so-called Sovereign Alliance including Iran, China, and Russia, and predicted that peace would break out but only after further heat.[N]
""")

merge(W + 'entities/faisal-bin-farhan.md', """## Regional diplomacy assessment (July 2024)

In the July 2, 2024 Badlands News Brief, contributor [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] wrote that "it's Faisal bin Farhan's show now," describing the Saudi foreign minister as working every diplomatic channel and speaking daily — per Foreign Ministry press releases — with members of what Badlands calls the Sovereign Alliance, including Iran, China, and Russia.[N] The commentary placed his statecraft at the center of a regional dynamic in which Arab states, aligned by the [[abraham-accords|Abraham Accords]], restrained escalation against Israel while the broader Muslim world stayed its hand, a posture the author predicted would eventually yield peace after a period of heightened conflict.[N]
""")

